WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.510
I could give an intro to Tolkien and his influence
on the fantasy genre - but instead, I’ll

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:11.080
quote beloved humorist and author, Terry Pratchett.
Said Pratchett in 2010: “[Tolkien] has become

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.650
a sort of mountain, appearing in all subsequent
fantasy in the way that Mt. Fuji appears so

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:20.350
often in Japanese prints. Sometimes it’s
big and up close. Sometimes it’s a shape

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.220
on the horizon. Sometimes it’s not there
at all, which means that the artist either

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:28.500
has made a deliberate decision against the
mountain, [...] or is in fact standing on

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.570
Mt. Fuji.” - Terry Pratchett

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:34.690
So Tolkien is widely regarded as the most
influential author on the fantasy genre…

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:40.000
period. But one of the less-discussed aspects
of his work is the way Tolkien used constructed

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.339
language in his writing.

00:00:41.339 --> 00:00:45.409
Nowadays authors are constantly making up
words and languages for the worlds they build,

00:00:45.409 --> 00:00:49.929
but Tolkien was unique in that he constructed
languages first, and then created worlds so

00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:53.289
his fictional languages would have somewhere
to live.

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:57.479
In 1958, he wrote in a letter to his son Christopher:
“[The Lord of the Rings] was an attempt

00:00:57.479 --> 00:01:02.600
to create a world in which a form of language
agreeable to my personal aesthetic might seem

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.600
real”

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.299
And In a lecture he gave to some fellow philologists
In 1931, Tolkien described his fondness for

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:14.040
constructing fictional languages as a “secret
vice” - almost like a shameful hobby. But

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.170
Tolkien’s “secret vice” wasn’t just
a strange quirk, it ended up as one of the

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.240
most influential aspects of everything he
wrote.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.380
Let’s talk a little bit about constructed
languages, or as they’re called in the Community,

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.420
a thing that exists, “ConLangs”

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.929
According to linguist Arika Okrent: “There
are trends, or eras, in language invention

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:40.820
that reflect the preoccupations of the surrounding
culture, and so, in a way, the history of

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.259
invented languages is a story about the way
we think about language”

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:49.210
According to Okrent, the history of constructed
languages is, for most of its history, extremely

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.929
utilitarian. Between the 17th-18th centuries,
the idea of a constructed Universal language

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:59.299
was thought of as a way of cataloging and
understanding the world, and is linked with

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:04.409
the development of science and mathematical
notation, as opposed to natural language which

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:08.119
was seen as flawed where it was not logical.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:14.310
But by the 19th Century, attention shifted
to the social use of language; in 1887, polish

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:20.260
linguist L. L. Zamenhof invented Esperanto,
to this day the most widely spoken constructed

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.370
language in the world. It was described as
an “easy and flexible language that would

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:30.930
serve as a universal second language to foster
peace and international understanding”

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:37.099
But Tolkien was not the first to invent language
in fiction – Sir Thomas More’s 1516 book

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:42.030
Utopia, Francis Godwin’s 1638 book Man in
the Moone, and Jonathan Swift’s Gulliver's

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.739
Travels, became important landmarks in the
early use of language invention. Swift created

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:52.299
names, place names and phrases in several
languages spoken of places like Lilliput and

00:02:52.299 --> 00:02:53.860
brobdingnag

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:58.120
But none of these examples reach the level
of complexity of Tolkien’s languages. According

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.189
to Okrent: “...these creations usually aren’t
languages so much as they are ideas, a bit

00:03:03.189 --> 00:03:08.689
of vocabulary, a few phrases. … They serve
the story, never the other way around… For

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:12.189
Tolkien, language creation was an art all
its own.”

00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:17.250
Tolkien intertwined myth and language to create,
in his words, an “illusion of historicity.”

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.269
From his earliest language writing in 1907
all the way to his death in the 1970’s,

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:26.450
Tolkien never stopped developing what he called
his ‘nexus of languages’”

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:30.650
Tolkien didn’t just see language creation
as a hobby, but an art, akin to writing fiction,

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:35.829
and saw the two is as intertwined.In his own
words: “For perfect construction of an art-language

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:40.680
it is found necessary to construct at least
in outline a mythology concomitant […]&nbsp; the

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.459
converse indeed is true -- your language construction
will breed a mythology.”

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:50.329
He notes that in contrast to more utilitarian
constructed languages like Esperanto, his

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:54.409
interest was in associating language invention
with pleasure.

00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:59.340
But Tolkien tended to downplay his languages
to friends and family, calling it his “secret

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:05.069
vice” and using such dismissive and self-deprecating
ways to describe them as “my nonsense fairy

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:10.109
language,” “unpublished inventions known
only to my family,” and “the shame-faced

00:04:10.109 --> 00:04:18.280
revelation of specimens of my own more considered
effort.”

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.920
By the time Tolkien started working on The
Hobbit in The Early 1930s he had already invented

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:28.000
the “Nexus of languages” which gave his
nomenclature, again in his words, a “coherence

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.940
and consistency” that he found lacking in
“other name inventors.”

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.630
The first instance we see of this nexus is
simply the mention of “Moria” -- Moria

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:43.540
is a Noldorin, later Sindarin or your basic
Elvish name meaning “Black Chasm”

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.630
We’d later also know it by its Dwarvish
name, Khazad-dûm

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:51.290
But while there is evidence of Tolkien's language
invention serving his nomenclature in The

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.810
Hobbit, it was not until the publication of
the first entry in the Lord of the Rings trilogy,

00:04:55.810 --> 00:05:00.970
The Fellowship of the Ring in 1954, that readers
were given the first longer samples of his

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.390
Elvish languages.

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:08.450
On page 80 of Fellowship, Frodo flexes his
language skills by speaking Elen síla lúmenn’

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:14.080
omentielvo, “A star Shines on the hour of
our meeting”, you go, Frodo, work those

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.680
college language credits.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.900
This is the first example of an Elvish language,
in this case the high Elven language of quenya,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.110
appearing as dialog in the text.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:27.830
Later in the same volume, some elves speak
it and we also get some poems - Galadriel’s

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:32.160
lament aka “Namárië” which means “farewell”

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:41.080
Language is a recurring theme throughout the
books -- even the tree-like ents not only

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:46.050
get a few “inaccurate” (Treebeard’s
words, not mine) sampling of their own extremely

00:05:46.050 --> 00:06:01.930
slow and thoughtful language, but also discuss
the way it differs from the languages of elves,

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.960
dwarves and men. It’s not only extremely
slow, it’s also highly detailed.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.410
Even the Elves, master linguists, could not
learn Old Entish.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:14.230
And in Return of the King we get appendices
that talk a LOT about the languages and the

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:19.600
“translation” (Tolkien acting as a historian,
not a fiction writer, tee hee)

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.560
Here he equivocates “Westron” - the common
language of men, with Modern English, and

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.450
Rohirric, the language of Rohan, with Old
English, and so on..

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:33.410
But Tolkien’s mythology is most meticulously
developed in The Silmarillion , which is Tolkien

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:38.320
in full self-indulgence mode. It was published
posthumously, so we don’t know what he would

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.060
have wanted the reader in on exactly, but
it’s the most detailed outlining of his

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.710
mythology that’s been published.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:48.940
So let’s talk specifics about this nexus
of languages.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.520
Sindarin - Spoken by the Grey Elves - basically
the “common tongue” of elves in middle

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.020
earth in the third age, when LotR takes place,
so Legolas, Elrond and Arwen - they speak

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:07.130
Sindarin. It has characteristics similar to
Welsh and other Celtic languages (such as

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.630
Irish, Manx, Scottish, and Cornish)

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:14.620
Quenya is the language of the Eldar after they return
to Middle Earth - so it’s the primary language

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.890
of high elves like Galadriel and Celeborn,
but the elves of Lothlórien still speak Sindarin

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:25.240
on the day to day. So it’s considered a
high, formal language - Aragorn speaks it

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:26.240
sometimes .

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:37.900
Quenya’s biggest real-world language influence
was Finnish, which was JRR’s fave, and its

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.640
use in Finnish epics like “The Kalevala”

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.680
Quenya focused on the use of open, long vowels,
and his sound combination rules emphasized

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:49.970
a softening of consonant stops; all elements
of Finnish phonetics

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.890
That said, many of the words in Quenya tend
to end in open vowels (e.g.. Norolinde, pirukendea,

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.470
nieninqea), whereas in Sindarin words tend
to end in consonants (hindar, Nebrachar, magradhaid)

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:02.280
giving Sindarin a different sound aesthetic
than Quenya. Quenya and Sindarin both descend

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.660
from a common ancestor, Quendian, because
Tolkien wanted his elvish languages to feel

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.580
lived in, and to evolve like real languages
do.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:15.060
So where English, German, Spanish, Irish,
Persian and Hindi all descend from the same

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.630
proto-langauge and are in the same language
family, so it goes with Elvish languages.

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:22.690
This is in contrast to--

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:28.290
Black speech is almost kind of a parody of
international auxiliary languages like Esperanto.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:34.639
Sauron “created” it to be its own evil
language inaccessible to outsiders - not an

00:08:34.639 --> 00:08:38.649
organic language within a language family
that evolved over time.

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:54.670
The most famous use of this, of course, the
inscription on the One Ring, even though it’s

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:56.970
written in an Elvish script (we’ll get to
that).

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:01.639
Weirdly, in-universe it’s mostly restricted
to Sauron’s evil higher-ups like the Nazgul

00:09:01.639 --> 00:09:15.879
that speak it - most orcs just speak like
a cockney variant on Westron (English)

00:09:15.879 --> 00:09:20.889
And then there’s the Dwarves and their Dwarvish
language, Khuzdul. Based partially on Semitic

00:09:20.889 --> 00:09:23.920
languages like Arabic and Hebrew, --

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.380
Yeah, about that...

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.480
Tolkien noted some similarities between Dwarves
and Jewish people: both were, in his words:

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:36.759
"at once natives and aliens in their habitations,
speaking the languages of the country, but

00:09:36.759 --> 00:09:41.230
with an accent due to their own private tongue…".&nbsp;
Tolkien stated that&nbsp; "[Dwarves’] words

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:44.250
are Semitic obviously, constructed to be Semitic."

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:47.250
So, yeah, maybe a little tone deaf.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:51.670
Khuzdul has its own script that is based on
nordic runes, and is&nbsp; derived from Cirth,

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:56.040
another invention of Tolkien. But in universe
not much is known about it, because the Dwarves

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:01.029
keep it ~secret~, so it has very little vocabulary
compared to Elvish languages

00:10:01.029 --> 00:10:05.199
Tengwar, the writing system used to write
Elvish and black speech, is utilized much

00:10:05.199 --> 00:10:12.640
more in terms of script. Most famous use - One
ring to rule them all/Ash nazg durbatulûk,

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:14.949
ash nazg gimbatul

00:10:14.949 --> 00:10:20.670
It has been described as a script that looks
like a "mixture of Hebrew, Greek, and Pitman's

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:25.240
shorthand,” but we can only speculate on
what writing systems influenced Tengwar. The

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:30.259
way it incorporates vowels reminds me of abugida
scripts like Devanagari, which is used for

00:10:30.259 --> 00:10:36.529
north indian languages like Hindi.
So given all we know about these languages,

00:10:36.529 --> 00:10:40.839
is it possible to speak Quenya and Sindarin?
No.

00:10:40.839 --> 00:10:46.300
The vocabulary, grammar, and syntax of Tolkien's
invented languages, even of Quenya and Sindarin,

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.839
are far too incomplete to allow its casual,
conversational, or quotidian use.

00:10:50.839 --> 00:10:55.680
So a lot of what you see in the movies is
guesswork, much of which was constructed by

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.600
linguist David Salo, who kinda filled in the
plugs in the DNA sequence gaps with frog DNA.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.410
Even before the publication of the final volume
of The Lord of the Rings, fans begin to write

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:09.999
to Tolkien to ask for more information about
his invented languages. And he actually indulged

00:11:09.999 --> 00:11:12.220
them… for a little while

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:15.749
Un...til they started calling his house in
the middle of the night and he had to move

00:11:15.749 --> 00:11:22.050
and remove his listing from the phone book
Starting in the 1960’s, Linguists and scholars

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:27.139
began to create societies dedicated to the
study of his languages, like the Elvish Linguistic

00:11:27.139 --> 00:11:31.630
Fellowship, which collabed w/ JRR’s son
Christopher in the 90’s to edit and publish

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:35.160
his father's numerous manuscripts of detailed
linguistic material.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:40.009
Ever since, there have been Tolkien language
bulletins, magazines, and societies popping

00:11:40.009 --> 00:11:42.420
up dedicated to studying his languages.

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:46.020
And they exploded in popularity with the release
of the lord of the rings films, starting in

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:51.520
2001.
And Tolkien’s influence lives on

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.279
Richard Adams’s Watership Down has the rabbit
language&nbsp; “Lapine” which is integrated

00:11:56.279 --> 00:12:01.000
throughout the text so that the reader absorbs
it through context clues, So when towards

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.759
the end of the book good rabbit tells bad
rabbit&nbsp; “Silflay hraka u enmeer rah”

00:12:06.759 --> 00:12:11.199
- effectively, $$%$##&amp;*(&amp;&amp;^%
- the reader understands it without needing

00:12:11.199 --> 00:12:12.199
it translated

00:12:12.199 --> 00:12:17.569
Nowadays, conlangs are in the popular mainstream.
As Star Trek was becoming A Thing just as

00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:25.220
LotR was exploding in popularity in the US,
Klingon - the most immediate (and obvious)

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:32.690
descendant, is now the most highly developed
conglang there is … that we know of...

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:39.390
And also has the highest number of speakers
for any fictional conlang (besides Esperanto, which

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.749
is still …. trying.)

00:12:41.749 --> 00:12:46.850
Now producers of movies and tv shows actually
hire professional linguists and members of

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:52.889
conlang societies to build languages for dialogue.
The Dothraki language in George RR Martin’s

00:12:52.889 --> 00:12:57.740
ASOIAF which Martin had only briefly outlined
in the novels was developed into a full functioning

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:59.790
language in Game of Thrones.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:04.460
And for Avatar, James Cameron hired linguist
Paul Frommer to create the Na’vi language

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:09.980
(largely based on Polynesian languages and
things that sound “tribal yet futuristic”

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.480
like the Papyrus font)

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:16.269
Even video games like Skyrim and Dragon Age
have their own language systems. In Skyrim

00:13:16.269 --> 00:13:19.990
learning to use the dragon language is a crucial
part of the gameplay.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.970
Tolkien’s work played a crucial role in
elevating language invention to a key part

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:28.579
of inventing imaginary worlds and elements
of invented language are now part and parcel

00:13:28.579 --> 00:13:30.360
of science fiction + fantasy.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.899
David Peterson, who fleshed out George R.
R. Martin’s Dothraki and High Valyrian languages

00:13:34.899 --> 00:13:41.180
from a few words to fully fleshed out conlangs,
claims that prior to 1991, people looked down

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:42.180
on conlangers…

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:47.480
But, post 2015, he says the conlang community
has really taken off… “There are conlangers

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:51.189
active on Tumblr, Twitter, and Facebook who
have no connection to any of the original

00:13:51.189 --> 00:13:55.930
communities--and they’re drawing inspiration
from languages that didn’t exist even five

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.000
years ago.” (15-17)

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.009
So it's kind of funny that Tolkien was so
shy about the fact that he had a hobby of

00:14:01.009 --> 00:14:06.290
inventing languages, since it ended up being
incredibly influential on not just fantasy

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:08.360
fiction but fiction in general.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:14.839
So don’t be ashamed of your “secret vice”
- post that cringe - you might accidentally

00:14:14.839 --> 00:14:16.119
invent a genre.

